namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup regions

\section regions-intro Introduction

\section regions-slicing Slicing and restriction

Images and sequences feature slicing and restriction capabilities,
two distinct mechanisms that allow for efficient manipulation of subregions.
Both are used to create regional views over the original data structure,
but they differ in the objects they return

Slicing returns owning views as instances of `Image` or `Sequence`.
They have a strided memory and their domains are necessarily regular, i.e., 1D intervals or ND axis-aligned boxes.
The slicing domain is created with a syntax analogous to Python's slicing:

- `Slice()` means to select all elements along a dimension;
- `Slice(index)` selects a single index;
- `Slice(start, stop, step)` selects elements from index `start` to index `stop - 1`.

Such constructs can be combined through `operator()` to create multi-dimensional slices.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp slicing

In contrast to slices, no assumptions are made on the memory layout of restrictions, therefore they support domains of any kind.
There are two kinds of restrictions:
- Patches, explicitely defined by their domain;
- Selections, defined by a predicate applied to the values of the parent view.

`Patch` and `Selection` are extremely lightweight classes that simply holds a domain or predicate and a pointer to the parent data.
The latter must remain valid during the lifetime of the restriction.

```
auto patch = Linx::Patch(image, any_domain);
assert(patch.domain() == any_domain);
assert(patch.parent() == image);
assert(patch.data() == image.data());
```

All the pixelwise operations, including random number generation, can be performed on restrictions.

\snippet{trimleft} regions_example.cpp pixelwise

Still, simple domains like boxes or slices can be used to create patches as well:

```
auto patch = Linx::Patch(image, Linx::Slice(1, 4)()(5));
assert(patch.n == 3);
```

*/

}
